WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:02.020
TOM SCOTT: The Youth Winter Olympic Games

00:00:02.020 --> 00:00:04.439
have brought me over to Lillehammer, in Norway.

00:00:04.439 --> 00:00:06.319
It's still early in the season here,

00:00:06.319 --> 00:00:08.060
so they are making snow.

00:00:08.060 --> 00:00:11.290
Because getting these slopes to competition
standard isn't easy.

00:00:11.290 --> 00:00:15.059
ROGER HJELMSTADSTUEN: Here in Hafjell we have
about 250 snow guns

00:00:15.059 --> 00:00:17.850
They're all controlled from a big control
room

00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:19.370
and they all are connected together.

00:00:19.370 --> 00:00:22.980
It's a lot about planning. To put the guns
at the right place.

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:27.740
It's about wind, if you put it on the wrong
side it's going to be a lot of snow in the

00:00:27.740 --> 00:00:28.050
trees.

00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:31.810
That's why we have two teams working night
and day

00:00:31.810 --> 00:00:35.739
the whole fall, the whole winter, to make
sure everything is perfect.

00:00:35.739 --> 00:00:39.539
TS: Here, in the snow gun, huge amounts of
water and air

00:00:39.539 --> 00:00:41.579
are being forced through tiny nozzles,

00:00:41.579 --> 00:00:44.519
creating a fine mist that freezes into fresh
powder.

00:00:44.519 --> 00:00:46.579
But while the weather is cold,

00:00:46.579 --> 00:00:49.260
the temperature is actually not quite below
freezing here.

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:51.789
So why does this water freeze into snow?

00:00:51.789 --> 00:00:53.579
Why doesn't it just... rain?

00:00:53.579 --> 00:00:54.819
Okay. Physics.

00:00:54.819 --> 00:00:59.179
As water evaporates, it sucks heat from the
surrounding environment.

00:00:59.179 --> 00:01:01.050
Put some water on your hand and blow on it,

00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:03.379
and the wet part will feel colder

00:01:03.379 --> 00:01:07.140
because that evaporating water is sucking
the heat away from your body.

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:08.570
That's what's going on here.

00:01:08.570 --> 00:01:10.200
Some of these water droplets evaporate,

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:12.490
so they drop the temperature around them.

00:01:12.490 --> 00:01:15.130
Which means other droplets might start to
freeze.

00:01:15.130 --> 00:01:19.110
And once that's started, you get what are
called nucleation sites:

00:01:19.110 --> 00:01:21.170
the start of snowflakes.

00:01:21.170 --> 00:01:23.180
And if it's not quite working efficiently
enough,

00:01:23.180 --> 00:01:25.400
then you can buy a kind of protein slurry.

00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:27.890
You add it, very diluted to the water,

00:01:27.890 --> 00:01:30.930
and suddenly every droplet is a nucleation
site

00:01:30.930 --> 00:01:32.280
and can form its own snowflake.

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:35.880
RH: To make snow, we have pipes for water
and pipes for air.

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:38.580
On top of that, every cannon needs a computer
cable.

00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:42.420
The water here in Hafjell comes from big lakes
at the top of the mountain

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:46.200
and from there it's natural pressure going
down.

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:49.180
That's the easiest and least expensive way.

00:01:49.180 --> 00:01:54.180
During a normal season, we use about 30,000
litres a minute.

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:58.540
It sounds a lot, but we have a big, big lake

00:01:58.540 --> 00:02:01.990
so during the whole season it's about 5-10cm
down

00:02:01.990 --> 00:02:04.860
and that's if no new water is coming in

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:08.420
so most of the time, it just looks like it's
not touched.

00:02:08.420 --> 00:02:12.620
TS: The drier the air is, the more water can
evaporate into it.

00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:15.930
So if the air is really, really dry, 0% humidity,

00:02:15.930 --> 00:02:18.159
you can make proper snow when the air temperature

00:02:18.159 --> 00:02:21.769
is well above freezing, anything up to 6°C.

00:02:21.769 --> 00:02:26.180
But if the air is humid, there is nowhere
for those droplets to evaporate to.

00:02:26.180 --> 00:02:29.540
At 100% humidity, when the air is saturated,

00:02:29.540 --> 00:02:32.790
the temperature has to be -2 or lower to make
snow.

00:02:32.790 --> 00:02:35.939
'Cos this isn't some kind of instant magic
freezer.

00:02:35.939 --> 00:02:38.480
The laws of thermodynamics pretty much make
that impossible.

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:42.340
Much as it'd be great to have a snow gun that
works in the middle of summer

00:02:42.340 --> 00:02:43.980
all you're going to end up with then...

00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:45.829
...is a rainmaker.

00:02:45.829 --> 00:02:49.060
RH: So, snowmaking is really important for
big contests,

00:02:49.060 --> 00:02:50.900
but it's also really important for just

00:02:50.900 --> 00:02:54.069
the average day here in Hafjell with normal
people.

00:02:54.069 --> 00:02:56.769
It's what we need to make this mountain work.

00:02:56.769 --> 00:02:59.029
Without it we'd be nothing. We need snow!

00:02:59.029 --> 00:03:02.010
TS: There's a load more videos over on the
Olympics channel

00:03:02.010 --> 00:03:04.340
and on my channel, go check 'em out, go subscribe,

00:03:04.340 --> 00:03:06.230
and thank you to the Youth Winter Olympic
Games

00:03:06.230 --> 00:03:07.959
for bringing me out here to Lillehammer.

00:03:08.900 --> 00:03:11.900
